Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.11.0
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.11.0 fixes a REGRESSION introduced by 0.10.0, where the floating window was mostly black. The archive #11 log shows the hosted scene losing its layers within about a second of being mounted and then never recovering: layers=0 for 220+ consecutive ticks while we kept writing settings a dozen times, with nothing coming back. Comparing the two builds side by side is what settles it. 0.9.1's recovery write contained setLevel:1 plus setDisplayConfiguration:, and it always pulled the picture back (that is where the flash came from). 0.10.0 judged, correctly, that those two calls are what make SpringBoard recompute the entire settings object - so it removed them from the routine write and kept only a "minimal" write of idleModeEnabled=NO, deactivationReasons=0 and the frame. The judgement was right; the conclusion was wrong. Those two calls are exactly what makes the render server build layers for the scene again, so the minimal write could never recover a real drop, and the archive #11 log proves it: not one of those writes brought the layers back. 0.11.0 therefore keeps 0.10.0's tightened condition - only layers<=0 or a presenter with zero hosted layers counts as black; deactivationReasons and contentState stay advisory-only log fields and are never allowed to trigger a write - and restores the full recovery write for genuine black only. Health is read-only; a real drop gets the whole set. Two further mistakes from 0.10.0 are corrected. First, its keepAliveGiveUpAfter=30 stopped writing altogether six seconds into a drop, which means permanently choosing a black screen for a state that must be rescued; this is replaced by backoff, where the interval doubles every 20 failed ticks up to keepAliveBackoffMaxMs but never stops. Second, black has two causes and 0.10.0 only recognised one: in the Filza round the scene itself was perfectly healthy (contentState=2, layers=2) while presenter.isHosting was 0, hostContainer was nil and presentationView had been resized to the full 430x932 screen. That is a hosting-side failure, and writing scene settings cannot touch it - it only donates one extra recompute. 0.11.0 now classifies first: layers>0 with hosted<=0 means repair the hosting only (re-activate the presenter and put the view back into the content area, touching no settings), while layers<=0 is what earns a settings write. The mount path is also deduplicated: the target content size is registered before P0 so the frame travels inside the same settings block, with a short-lived memo suppressing the immediately following duplicate write, cutting two full recomputes per mount down to one. New keys: keepAliveHardWrite (default true; set it to false to reproduce the 0.10.0 black screen for A/B), keepAliveBackoffAfter (50) and keepAliveBackoffMaxMs (2000). Changed: keepAliveGiveUpAfter 30 -> 0 (0 means never give up), keepAliveGraceMs 1500 -> 600. keepAliveSoft stays false.
Maintainer: QingC12 <QingC12@users.noreply.github.com>
Author: QingC12
Section: Tweaks
Depends: firmware (>= 15.0)
